1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,140
Effective memory management is very important in multi-programming system.

2
00:00:14,140 --> 00:00:25,000
To improve CPU utilization, multiple processes need to be loaded into memory in order to make

3
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:33,200
sure a reasonable supply of ready processes to consume available CPU time.

4
00:00:33,200 --> 00:00:40,600
So we begin this part of lecture by understanding how a process gets executed.

5
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:46,280
Next, we look at different memory management approaches.

6
00:00:46,280 --> 00:00:52,300
So in general, there are two different types of memory management approaches.

7
00:00:52,300 --> 00:00:56,040
One type is called contiguous allocation.

8
00:00:56,040 --> 00:01:01,500
So where the contiguous memory space is allocated to a process.

9
00:01:01,500 --> 00:01:12,740
So under contiguous memory allocation, we have a fixed allocation and dynamic partitioning.

10
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:26,480
In contrast to the contiguous memory allocation, so for paging and segmentation, where non-contiguous memory space will be allocated to a process.

11
00:01:26,480 --> 00:01:29,480
So we'll explain all these concepts.

12
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:41,220
So fixed partitioning, dynamic partitioning, paging and segmentation in details under this part of lecture.

13
00:01:41,220 --> 00:01:45,220
Now let's look at the storage hierarchy first.

14
00:01:45,220 --> 00:01:51,220
So the diagram here shows the storage hierarchy of a computer.

15
00:01:51,220 --> 00:01:57,960
So at the top of the hierarchy, so register, cache and the memory.

16
00:01:57,960 --> 00:02:03,680
So this commonly referred to as primary storage.

17
00:02:03,680 --> 00:02:10,780
Then below this, there are flash disks and traditional hard disks.

18
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:15,780
So this commonly referred to as secondary storage.

19
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:20,520
And at bottom, there are remote secondary storage.

20
00:02:20,520 --> 00:02:22,520
You can look at here.

21
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:32,520
So from top to bottom, the capacity will be increased, but the access time will be also increased.

22
00:02:32,520 --> 00:02:37,240
At the top hierarchy, the register and the cache.

23
00:02:37,240 --> 00:02:41,980
So these are managed by management by the CPU.

24
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:43,480
So this is our CPU.

25
00:02:43,480 --> 00:02:46,980
So this is not managed by operating system.

26
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:51,980
So the OIS will manage the main memory.

27
00:02:51,980 --> 00:02:57,980
And OIS will also manage the disk, the secondary storage.

28
00:02:57,980 --> 00:03:04,980
And for the topics that are going to be covered in the second half of the lecture.

29
00:03:04,980 --> 00:03:10,720
And for this part of the lecture, the memory organization.

